#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133497 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e023193-7494-5e3a-bd6e-e15ae0e9a1bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "105a5a4a-2fea-54bb-97c6-c2a700b05f61" :question/math-academy-id "e-5548" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle {x}(t), {y}(t) \\rangle = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector  $p(t) = 1 - t^2.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${p}(t) = 1-t^2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=0,\\qquad \np(2)= -3,\\qquad \np(3)=-8.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|p(t)\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(2)p(2)+p(3)p(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}+[p(3)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+9+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{73}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133498 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "090d6747-55e2-548b-84f2-88f6c732518f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Continuous Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "5aafdd7d-939a-5690-ac4d-46b554524d75" :question/math-academy-id "e-5549" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)= 2t-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle  f(t),  f(t) \\rangle}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}-2{t}^{2}+t)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-2+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{3}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133499 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "466fb505-1ff3-5b27-a5f7-0ea5b31b2927" :knowledge-point/title "Normalizing a Vector in an Inner Product Space"}] :question/id #uuid "d279bb1d-754e-5044-9a01-d4c86f30b269" :question/math-academy-id "e-5550" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,\\pi\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)= 3t.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle  f(t),  f(t) \\rangle}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{(3t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }9{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| f(t) \\|  = \\sqrt{3\\pi^3} = \\pi \\sqrt{3\\pi}.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $\\| f(t) \\| \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi \\sqrt{3\\pi }}\\cdot 3t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\sqrt{3}}{\\pi \\sqrt{\\pi }}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133552 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92053541-63fa-5cca-b3d8-0f4864c31051" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/id #uuid "35e7ce1a-b2cc-50dd-8bf7-24d20e55d623" :question/math-academy-id "e-5908" :question/problem "An inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle =  2x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 3x_3 y_3.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/assets/1e710ca7b3b7bea626de36181a30869b0f114a4497843c6b6ea8b8966a91d208.png) under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $\\mathbf v$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18+4+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e023193-7494-5e3a-bd6e-e15ae0e9a1bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9246526-6397-51af-84ff-fd20e1968f7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334594} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4194c8-dda6-52a9-9f18-3b52b97d4b7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334595} {:answer/id #uuid "fa06301a-1535-56c0-afde-4e1d6cc066c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334596} {:answer/id #uuid "8099fbd5-73ae-5375-8669-a07d81e63522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334597} {:answer/id #uuid "bfcd88d2-e52c-53c0-b0f4-10496abe8f45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa06301a-1535-56c0-afde-4e1d6cc066c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334596} :answer-field/id #uuid "fca14bd0-084b-5548-aa13-6fcf9ac9ac71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dea39803-23bf-5454-93d0-89ff1b7931a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-141169" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=2-3t+{t}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=2-3t+{t}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=0,\\,p(0)=2,\\,p(-1)=6.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(0)p(0)+p(-1)p(-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(0)]^{2}+[p(-1)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+4+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "090d6747-55e2-548b-84f2-88f6c732518f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba06921c-997b-516d-8291-540cf4b031aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308514} {:answer/id #uuid "380a9d83-dd4c-5f2e-beac-af7ddc86f847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308515} {:answer/id #uuid "826dff1e-eda2-541a-bfbc-fb1f4f6f09f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308516} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc2c4c9-5baf-5a56-8da9-d4b1cfbe8957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308517} {:answer/id #uuid "68795571-006f-58aa-ae3f-9ce848f8b9a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "826dff1e-eda2-541a-bfbc-fb1f4f6f09f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308516} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ceeabe5-4f09-5832-9388-27a782316d67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0f46084-f755-54b3-bb66-e1e926747894" :question/math-academy-id "q-141170" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=1-t.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-{t}^{2}+t)|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1+1)-(\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}-(-1)^{2}+(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{3}}=2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "466fb505-1ff3-5b27-a5f7-0ea5b31b2927" :knowledge-point/title "Normalizing a Vector in an Inner Product Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eacab1e4-8780-58a1-bee7-2ccbc24c1be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334601} {:answer/id #uuid "ba99b293-2d2e-5988-9135-d30a447404a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334602} {:answer/id #uuid "70363edc-5c98-5acd-9e0e-020072de6b0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334603} {:answer/id #uuid "25a72321-510d-5011-bdad-cda79db86fd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334604} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f2b646-d45b-5956-b520-550c26e3cfc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3f2b646-d45b-5956-b520-550c26e3cfc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334605} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f05ae56-af1f-5e7f-bdaa-ec32824f1103" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18ecc628-846f-52e0-b4d5-bd9869c51840" :question/math-academy-id "q-141174" :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector  $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4+3+1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e023193-7494-5e3a-bd6e-e15ae0e9a1bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c23f59b5-0270-554b-b21d-7135020eb1e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132399} {:answer/id #uuid "d067854f-6979-5ffd-9d2e-61cf11565c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132400} {:answer/id #uuid "025758a7-ccaf-5d1f-9884-2859f0d8f65a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132401} {:answer/id #uuid "67b481ab-bcb6-5c73-8714-006cd803facd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132402} {:answer/id #uuid "6dda4e19-cbc2-5eee-861d-fa9b7db78cd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d067854f-6979-5ffd-9d2e-61cf11565c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132400} :answer-field/id #uuid "55409861-12e6-5c4d-bc3a-180892e14f53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d534868-ebb8-5365-85d3-82c31abacac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-42726" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2)+x(3)y(3).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=1-2t.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=1-2t,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=-1,\\,p(2)=-3,\\,p(3)=-5.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(2)p(2)+p(3)p(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}+[p(3)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+9+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92053541-63fa-5cca-b3d8-0f4864c31051" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff259840-0fb8-5d40-bfdf-e0a21b1dc174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334608} {:answer/id #uuid "272fe965-ca5a-5092-b534-d27f749f26a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334609} {:answer/id #uuid "2a0255bb-fc8d-5c44-9bb5-9c90d39b6453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334610} {:answer/id #uuid "42f8451e-7a70-59e3-8741-a6ec21148a7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334611} {:answer/id #uuid "4fccd52c-fa00-5dd2-9b1b-f34b259ed99c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff259840-0fb8-5d40-bfdf-e0a21b1dc174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334608} :answer-field/id #uuid "b598a9c3-f90a-5e10-93e9-72bf44c4d7dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "900ff124-b9e7-56d6-99ac-b7a805a09450" :question/math-academy-id "q-42733" :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (-2\\cdot (-2))+2\\cdot (0\\cdot 0)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+12+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "090d6747-55e2-548b-84f2-88f6c732518f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97bdf9a8-ffaf-5d0b-9425-c755dda47e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132411} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b6ec19-50d3-5862-ae2f-84f8bf6691f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{π}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132412} {:answer/id #uuid "efe6343d-112b-5436-94fe-badccc0c01aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3π}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132413} {:answer/id #uuid "17afa45b-bbc8-5766-af24-69a971e6ce2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132414} {:answer/id #uuid "1afe2fbd-2474-5ce7-8a18-c55f15a0687d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9b6ec19-50d3-5862-ae2f-84f8bf6691f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{π}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132412} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0e3d6ac-1c0b-5a74-beca-2baaad2089e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f27dcc08-cb74-544e-b846-2c5aa548a3f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-42734" :question/problem "Let $V=C[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[\\pi ,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{t}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{4}){|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi }{4})-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =(\\pi +0)-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡t=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "466fb505-1ff3-5b27-a5f7-0ea5b31b2927" :knowledge-point/title "Normalizing a Vector in an Inner Product Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dfca35e-08fe-5bb9-95a9-0f5bac654aa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334615} {:answer/id #uuid "c4992185-5ba9-5f5c-a7da-2056e928ccf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334616} {:answer/id #uuid "276ad0ba-d974-5558-81bb-fd06f1d2eb03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334617} {:answer/id #uuid "ae09c23e-4833-5bda-9f77-cac16120ff3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334618} {:answer/id #uuid "8738e785-c246-5d60-8da4-b5069be4fdea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "276ad0ba-d974-5558-81bb-fd06f1d2eb03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334617} :answer-field/id #uuid "748265bd-ac3c-5cab-8148-81ff2d57a7ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a226526-c78d-5b45-912b-bc1bae613541" :question/math-academy-id "q-42739" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)=t-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{(t-1)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-{t}^{2}+t)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-1+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $‖f(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{t-1}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(t-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "466fb505-1ff3-5b27-a5f7-0ea5b31b2927" :knowledge-point/title "Normalizing a Vector in an Inner Product Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad991df1-d65d-5dd0-993b-a41b1b0506a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(2 + t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132417} {:answer/id #uuid "00d82f81-e661-5ad5-83ea-b2fb5edbe2ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(2 + t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132418} {:answer/id #uuid "0493056c-f245-55a1-9c75-5d1a97b1c15d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{50}(2 + t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132419} {:answer/id #uuid "518ec4a9-e4ea-5643-84b6-3ce33513d026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}(2 + t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132420} {:answer/id #uuid "7e08f1cb-2b3c-5192-a8cd-e31ab51eebd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(2 + t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00d82f81-e661-5ad5-83ea-b2fb5edbe2ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(2 + t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132418} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d7502c-5e95-5cb1-b7bc-542be514f2ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "443381c1-3014-5c89-915a-d8f5ec0d3345" :question/math-academy-id "q-42741" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{1}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $1,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nNormalize the  vector  $p(t)=2+t.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=2+t,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=3,\\,p(2)=4.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),p(t)⟩ & =[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =9+16 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{25}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $p(t)$, we divide $p(t)$ by its norm $‖p(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{p(t)}{‖p(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+t}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2+t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92053541-63fa-5cca-b3d8-0f4864c31051" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fb74c47-bb4c-5e59-831b-f4db6f207238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308528} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd88ade-f649-591f-a667-bace87399085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308529} {:answer/id #uuid "7209ba9c-9602-5431-9c0f-4d49cc1732fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308530} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ee8927-137c-5f76-9b8a-6ae96460c881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308531} {:answer/id #uuid "a33baad8-9adb-510a-9d9f-94fe9cf0f431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a33baad8-9adb-510a-9d9f-94fe9cf0f431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308532} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a422833-c88d-5729-a704-8c93d9261cb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e498321f-6c47-59d7-91ad-fafbad52af14" :question/math-academy-id "q-43908" :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+7{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot 3+4\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (-2))+7\\cdot (0\\cdot 0)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "090d6747-55e2-548b-84f2-88f6c732518f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "890dfec2-e35c-51f0-84e0-fb7923b16b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{103}{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308535} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3741ae-1348-5340-95f0-a2bed477867b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{33}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308536} {:answer/id #uuid "63053eb2-3740-5954-987e-c4e4e26f1bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{103}{30}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308537} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3d9257-414a-59e1-9394-a8b362afbd3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308538} {:answer/id #uuid "fc84eb3d-ef95-5085-89de-fd40f21602cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63053eb2-3740-5954-987e-c4e4e26f1bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{103}{30}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308537} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3da0305-2f92-5dc2-bb82-4c3ad9ce07e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e5ddf58-998d-561f-b859-a4149f98cf8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-43920" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=2t+\\sqrt{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+\\sqrt{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+4t\\sqrt{t}+t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{8{t}^{2}\\sqrt{t}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{5}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{103}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{\\frac{103}{30}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "090d6747-55e2-548b-84f2-88f6c732518f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d56d056-3b2b-5686-92dd-f3b930116b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi +1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334622} {:answer/id #uuid "7f71ce10-a80e-52a2-8473-98f666dd5799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi +\\frac{1}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334623} {:answer/id #uuid "46866437-8698-5100-b296-08bd1c937daa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334624} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f2a67f-8d20-59ca-b6c0-78678601f19a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334625} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd860b4-a09f-50ad-863b-324c4efc37a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7f2a67f-8d20-59ca-b6c0-78678601f19a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334625} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4be0730-02f0-5964-95e5-fc118bc62c9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22161582-39f7-5f14-a415-005aa76ca21c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43921" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\,{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{4}){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{4})-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{4}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡t=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92053541-63fa-5cca-b3d8-0f4864c31051" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022812d0-4ae0-5c73-938e-99500be513f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334629} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5ac14d-5a96-589d-9fa9-9b50d03d29a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334630} {:answer/id #uuid "b49e2a33-e5da-5f82-8079-c89fcd1a9715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334631} {:answer/id #uuid "a45286e1-e8ab-5db2-89f9-eede4d464360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334632} {:answer/id #uuid "97a067bb-d93c-54f0-8d68-c059e77a9727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd5ac14d-5a96-589d-9fa9-9b50d03d29a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334630} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2fb0be-0186-5c01-89da-5f4a8fb2a577" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da16d8f2-5169-5150-a4bd-05ef9881ea9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43929" :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (-1))+4\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+2+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92053541-63fa-5cca-b3d8-0f4864c31051" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75123481-23ce-5398-b68c-1355b8289060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334636} {:answer/id #uuid "f421beed-3ecf-5c65-ad4e-fe4955afd29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334637} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef57544-e763-58e1-be2a-538c7f4a5d42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334638} {:answer/id #uuid "1750de8f-8e48-51a6-8722-f478e3729331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334639} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad86efb-3382-5097-9983-807a7c2af578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f421beed-3ecf-5c65-ad4e-fe4955afd29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334637} :answer-field/id #uuid "9981fd98-f2c1-5138-b65e-54e5f91bc98a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a21f236-07e4-565e-94ff-73b535a01e0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-97410" :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+3\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (4\\cdot 4)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8+9+32} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92053541-63fa-5cca-b3d8-0f4864c31051" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d972ed08-7aac-5a90-b5ab-eb8eb907ed35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308556} {:answer/id #uuid "da744d9b-ef30-53a5-bb4c-135bf8e5357e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308557} {:answer/id #uuid "aa339327-5e52-5d2a-9032-1e4d16ee4128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308558} {:answer/id #uuid "b70c44a5-1bcd-5ca1-b38e-32ce49bc0605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308559} {:answer/id #uuid "13fdf335-5977-5424-912e-dcba9a706e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da744d9b-ef30-53a5-bb4c-135bf8e5357e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308557} :answer-field/id #uuid "92ecd833-0dff-512b-9685-3eab07ff25a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5daddfb3-e7f8-5c06-afbf-1d755d1586e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-97413" :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+(-3)\\cdot (-3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18+8+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e023193-7494-5e3a-bd6e-e15ae0e9a1bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "750755aa-f8a3-5df4-a17e-a8d5870332ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334643} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca2c068-09ad-5d1e-839d-9360e6a66e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334644} {:answer/id #uuid "09883923-2498-5111-860f-c25f2316de28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334645} {:answer/id #uuid "0daa0b98-90e6-5a8f-9b1c-06d96399bc43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334646} {:answer/id #uuid "b96e8328-ba05-5880-9ee9-67946f7f46a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ca2c068-09ad-5d1e-839d-9360e6a66e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334644} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1102dc6-9fea-5714-9894-b2c091908038" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65d60515-7767-5b6f-afb5-20fa2b5f3e11" :question/math-academy-id "q-97417" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=2t-{t}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=2t-{t}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=1,\\,p(0)=0,\\,p(-1)=-3.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(0)p(0)+p(-1)p(-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(0)]^{2}+[p(-1)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e023193-7494-5e3a-bd6e-e15ae0e9a1bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcb93c50-b6a4-5ed0-ba6e-c1f5258956ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133293} {:answer/id #uuid "04fdfe8a-728b-5d28-bf40-3c2920036919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133294} {:answer/id #uuid "47425b26-9946-5769-922a-472f69b0c2c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133295} {:answer/id #uuid "6e83664f-4798-5180-93bd-0aa51180ebdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133296} {:answer/id #uuid "710e8117-3952-528b-865e-9de244ca3297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04fdfe8a-728b-5d28-bf40-3c2920036919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133294} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8bf7216-e5ce-5571-8b5d-92a21be1635d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17b9d976-83b8-5275-b6d4-e009224e5ca4" :question/math-academy-id "q-97420" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(0)y(0)+x(2)y(2)+x(3)y(3).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=4-2t+{t}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=4-2t+{t}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(0)=4,\\,p(2)=4,\\,p(3)=7.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(0)p(0)+p(2)p(2)+p(3)p(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(0)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}+[p(3)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(7)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16+49} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e023193-7494-5e3a-bd6e-e15ae0e9a1bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fbacd75-9738-56dc-b6b5-921fcb119720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308577} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2bdcea-f2f0-5a18-8b5a-3279b03a9b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308578} {:answer/id #uuid "aefb5a38-b4d0-556c-a547-8ad1308074f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308579} {:answer/id #uuid "2b437129-9914-52c7-aee1-27516a9012f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308580} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff35215-6916-5a16-940d-5a2bcbd67ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aefb5a38-b4d0-556c-a547-8ad1308074f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308579} :answer-field/id #uuid "be1ea524-178e-573e-a3fc-1d252368aebb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "294160f3-8768-53a9-8a25-c924f493cc11" :question/math-academy-id "q-97422" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=4-{t}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=4-{t}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=3,\\,p(0)=4,\\,p(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(0)p(0)+p(-1)p(-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(0)]^{2}+[p(-1)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "090d6747-55e2-548b-84f2-88f6c732518f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector in an Inner Product Space of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83109460-cef8-5910-80c3-adc60d429565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{68}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334650} {:answer/id #uuid "780bef2d-2dc6-55eb-b3d3-eaab589560c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{34}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334651} {:answer/id #uuid "fd586785-2ba5-562c-93f1-49932febf369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{32}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334652} {:answer/id #uuid "1f144e6a-a021-58eb-a667-588689647fcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334653} {:answer/id #uuid "2a176b9b-3ad3-54dc-a5cd-8dcba04bcc49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{56}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83109460-cef8-5910-80c3-adc60d429565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{68}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334650} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc263b3a-4faa-58ce-9317-8fec92f98b04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1a41dda-d8f9-5690-92f2-1ed2d1f5820d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97715" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,4]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,4],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{4}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\sqrt{t}+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(\\sqrt{t}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(t+2\\sqrt{t}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{4t\\sqrt{t}}{3}+t)|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =(8+\\frac{32}{3}+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{68}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{\\frac{68}{3}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "466fb505-1ff3-5b27-a5f7-0ea5b31b2927" :knowledge-point/title "Normalizing a Vector in an Inner Product Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e830c27-e5cb-5916-8f67-c7aef0e53df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308605} {:answer/id #uuid "7a4b9b81-5a16-5bb5-9d00-e657245d3072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308606} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a0765c-c75c-53a4-8107-43ded4173630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308607} {:answer/id #uuid "46dbf34c-4240-5507-808e-b3b6c54cb1a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308608} {:answer/id #uuid "a38e81e7-84e3-53b7-925e-3eb09ea6ad9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e830c27-e5cb-5916-8f67-c7aef0e53df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308605} :answer-field/id #uuid "6387a3c0-8fd8-5255-a956-ebb777c4aa46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d095eb6a-2ae3-5699-bca7-a936ad3d6b9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97725" :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =3\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =12+1+3 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{16}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "466fb505-1ff3-5b27-a5f7-0ea5b31b2927" :knowledge-point/title "Normalizing a Vector in an Inner Product Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7de7c998-a09f-5750-a9b5-aa4dc8b1ccf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308612} {:answer/id #uuid "cb20cb48-9c1d-55d1-8a85-5b808463d433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308613} {:answer/id #uuid "b860d49b-45b1-56a0-b923-5dac24c33f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308614} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9ebcc3-4857-583e-b281-074cb0eb6d6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{22}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308615} {:answer/id #uuid "b15945df-fbbb-5ef3-b4de-9e50b2425110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b860d49b-45b1-56a0-b923-5dac24c33f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308614} :answer-field/id #uuid "b121e9c3-f2c7-5ee2-bf76-6405555aff01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fae5da3-e57f-5671-91de-202da267e1a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-97726" :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+(-3)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =18+8+9 \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
